// SECURITY -- CLOSED. A DIFFERENT gap from the container-mutation family
// above: a closure reaching a container's authority through a HOF, when the
// HOF's OWN callback argument is a NAMED FUNCTION that itself forwards a
// caller-supplied fn-value (`fn invoke(f: fn() -> str) -> str { return f() }`)
// -- `map(tools, invoke)` -- rather than an inline lambda.
//
// Root cause: `resolve_closure_caps`'s `Identifier` arm, for a bare
// reference to a named function, returned that function's OWN declared/
// inferred capability set (`working[name]`) unconditionally. That set only
// reflects gated BUILTINS the function calls directly -- it says nothing
// about what authority flows through a HOT parameter `name` itself forwards
// (`invoke`'s own `f` is hot, called directly). So handing `invoke` to
// `map` as a bare VALUE (not calling it with a concrete argument yet) was
// attributed `Known(empty)`, silently dropping the fact that `invoke` will
// eventually be called once per element of `tools` -- which is exactly
// where the real secret closure lives.
//
// Fix (`kryos-capabilities/src/checker.rs`, `resolve_closure_caps`): before
// returning a named function's declared capability set, check whether the
// function ITSELF has any hot parameter (`hot_params.get(name)` non-empty).
// If so, an unapplied reference to it cannot be vouched for here (we don't
// yet know what it will be called with) -- fall back to `Unknown` ->
// `Capability::All`, the same conservative default already used for every
// other unresolvable fn-value provenance.
//
// CASCADE, measured (not assumed): grepped `compiler/stdlib`,
// `compiler/self-host`, and `examples` for a bare-identifier (non-lambda)
// argument to any `std::iter` HOF -- zero real occurrences; every actual
// callback argument in this codebase is an inline lambda. Also confirmed
// live that the FUNCTIONALLY EQUIVALENT lambda-wrapped form
// (`map(tools, |f| invoke(f))`) was ALREADY rejected before this fix (a
// pre-existing, unrelated conservatism: a lambda's own bound parameter used
// to invoke a hot-forwarding function inline resolves to `Unknown` via the
// original closure-fix's enforcement, regardless of this change) -- so this
// fix closes an INCONSISTENCY (naming the same adapter function explicitly
// was silently MORE permissive than the equivalent lambda form) rather than
// introducing a new restriction on previously-working code. Full gate suite
// (conformance 58/58, tier1+tier2 GREEN including `strict_caps`/`examples`/
// `examples_e2e`, bootstrap 16/16) re-verified GREEN after this change.
//
// ACCEPTANCE (met): REJECTED (E0507, requiring `all`) under BOTH
// `--capabilities-mode=inferred` (default) and `--strict-capabilities`.
// Verified BOTH ways: reverting the `hot_params` check in
// `resolve_closure_caps`'s `Identifier` arm and rebuilding makes this
// compile clean and print the secret again.
use std::iter::{map}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}
fn invoke(f: fn() -> str) -> str {
    return f()
}
@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let mut tools: [fn() -> str] = []
    tools = push(tools, reader)
    deny!(fs:read) {
        let results = map(tools, invoke)
        println("HOF-FORWARD LEAK: " + results[0])
    }
}
